Mark XXVII: device injection (scan + install + bundle)

One command installs UMBRA on another device.

- inject.py: jarvis inject scan sweeps the local /24 on telling ports
  (22 ssh, 445 smb, 5555 adb, 8775 UMBRA API) with a threaded TCP sweep
  (stdlib; ICMP needs raw sockets) and prints host + OS guess +
  install feasibility. jarvis inject install builds the exact plan for
  windows (scp + ssh silent installer), linux (scp + bash installer,
  optional systemd --user unit) or android (adb install -r), prints it
  with --dry-run (ZERO network calls), and executes only with --yes.
  jarvis inject bundle writes an offline installer zip: wheel (built on
  demand), UMBRA.exe when present, generated install-umbra.ps1/.sh,
  README, and the PUBLIC key text - never any private material
  (no_secrets_guard refuses .pem/.key/private members).
- Safety model: plans are pure data; runner.py is the single execution
  point (argument-list subprocesses, shell=False, no shell parser ever
  sees target or plan text). Targets are regex-validated to a strict
  [user@]host charset before any argv exists; remote ssh commands are
  module constants. Passwords are never asked for or stored (SSH
  keys/agent only); root/admin targets refused without --i-know;
  WinRM not implemented - the plan prints the manual command instead
  (noted assumption). Thin wrappers: scripts/inject.sh + inject.ps1.
- Tests: sweep finds a live loopback listener; plans for all three OSes;
  unsafe-target and root guards; bundle contents (installers + public
  key + README, no key material, guard refuses a planted .pem); dry-run
  prints the plan; --yes required to execute.
- Version 3.14.0.
